			<text class="d-alabel d-alabel--hero d-mid" x="340" y="128" style="font-size:14px;letter-spacing:.04em"
				>BROKER</text
			>
			<rect class="d-chip d-chip--green" x="278" y="146" width="124" height="26" rx="13"></rect>
			<text class="d-ctext d-ctext--green d-mid" x="340" y="164">home/temp</text>
			<rect class="d-actor" x="524" y="40" width="138" height="44" rx="10"></rect>
			<text class="d-alabel d-mid" x="593" y="67">Dashboard</text>
			<rect class="d-actor" x="524" y="120" width="138" height="44" rx="10"></rect>
			<text class="d-alabel d-mid" x="593" y="147">Data logger</text>
			<rect class="d-actor" x="524" y="200" width="138" height="44" rx="10"></rect>
			<text class="d-alabel d-mid" x="593" y="227">Phone app</text>
			<path class="d-line" d="M156 148 L253 148" marker-end="url(#arGray)"></path>
			<text class="d-tiny" x="176" y="140">publish</text>
			<path class="d-line d-line--green" d="M424 148 C474 148 472 62 521 62" marker-end="url(#arGreen)"></path>
			<path class="d-line d-line--green" d="M424 148 L521 142" marker-end="url(#arGreen)"></path>
			<path class="d-line d-line--green" d="M424 148 C474 148 472 222 521 222" marker-end="url(#arGreen)"></path>
			<text class="d-tiny" x="464" y="140" style="fill:#4f7d63">copy</text>
			<text class="d-note d-mid" x="340" y="270">One message in → a copy to every subscriber</text>
			<text class="d-note d-mid" x="340" y="290" style="fill:#8a8d94"
				>Publisher and subscribers share only the topic — never each other</text
			>
		</svg>
	</MqttDiagram>

	<h2>Why MQTT is used for IoT</h2>
	<p>
		MQTT was designed for constrained devices and unreliable networks, which is exactly the environment most IoT fleets
		live in. Its advantages:
	</p>
	<LearnFeatureGrid features={whyFeatures} />

	<h2>MQTT vs HTTP</h2>
	<p>
		HTTP is request/response: a client asks, the server answers, and the connection is typically short-lived. That's a
		poor fit for pushing frequent, small updates to many devices. MQTT keeps a long-lived connection open and pushes
		messages the instant they're published, with far less per-message overhead. See <a href="/mqtt/mqtt-vs-http/"
			>MQTT vs HTTP</a
		> for a fuller comparison. For moving that device data into backend analytics pipelines, MQTT is often paired with a streaming
		platform — see <a href="/mqtt/mqtt-vs-kafka/">MQTT vs Kafka</a>.
	</p>

	<h2>Core MQTT concepts</h2>
	<p>Once the pub/sub basics click, these are the concepts worth knowing next:</p>
	<ul>
		<li><a href="/mqtt/mqtt-broker/">MQTT broker</a> — the central server that routes every message.</li>
		<li><a href="/mqtt/mqtt-client/">MQTT clients</a> — the devices and apps that publish and subscribe.</li>
		<li><a href="/mqtt/publish-subscribe/">Publish/subscribe</a> — the messaging model MQTT is built on.</li>
		<li><a href="/mqtt/topics/">Topics &amp; wildcards</a> — how messages are addressed and matched.</li>
		<li><a href="/mqtt/mqtt-connection/">Connection (CONNECT)</a> — how a client opens a session.</li>
		<li><a href="/mqtt/mqtt-client-id/">Client ID</a> — how the broker identifies each client.</li>
		<li><a href="/mqtt/mqtt-packets/">MQTT packets</a> — the control packets clients and brokers exchange.</li>
		<li>
			<a href="/mqtt/qos/">Quality of Service (QoS)</a> — at-most-once, at-least-once, exactly-once delivery.
		</li>
		<li><a href="/mqtt/retained-messages/">Retained messages</a> — the last known value on a topic.</li>
		<li><a href="/mqtt/last-will/">Last Will &amp; Testament</a> — signalling ungraceful disconnects.</li>
		<li><a href="/mqtt/persistent-session/">Persistent sessions</a> — queuing messages for offline clients.</li>
		<li><a href="/mqtt/mqtt-5/">MQTT 5.0</a> — the latest version and what it adds.</li>
		<li><a href="/mqtt/shared-subscriptions/">Shared subscriptions</a> — load-balancing consumers.</li>
		<li><a href="/mqtt/security/">Security</a> — TLS, authentication, and authorization.</li>
	</ul>
</MqttTopicLayout>
